-- title:
--   Trivial Weil pairing for classes reducing into the torus
-- statement:
--   The statement is formulated inside a large frame describing the semistable model of $X_1(Mp)$ at $p$, its Picard scheme, and the dictionaries between divisor classes and scheme-theoretic points. The hypotheses are named and grouped below; none is passed over, and the content of several groups is summarised.
--
--   **Arithmetic base.** A prime $p$, a natural number $M$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$, a field $L$ of characteristic zero which is a $p$-cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb Q$, and $\zeta \in L$ a primitive $p$-th root of unity (`hζ`). Further, $K$ is an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ with `hK` asserting $K =$ [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb Q$. The ring $A$ is a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$, with $p$ in its maximal ideal (`hAp`) and $\zeta$ in the image of $A$ (`hζA`), acting on $K$ compatibly with the tower $A \subseteq L \subseteq K$. Finally $j \in K$ is nonzero and its Laurent expansion is the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) transported to $L$ (`hj`). The relevant curve over $A$ is the two-chart model [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252), which is assumed proper.
--
--   **The special fibre and its two components.** $k$ is an algebraically closed $A$-algebra of characteristic $p$; $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ are proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral; $i_1, i_2$ are closed immersions of $C_1$, $C_2$ into the base change of the two-chart model along $A \to k$, over $k$. The hypothesis `hcover` states that every point of that base change lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` states that the scheme-theoretic intersection `pullback i₁.1 i₂.1` is reduced, and `hn`, `hn0` name $n > 0$ as its number of points.
--
--   **Sections and relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ data.** $\varepsilon$ is a section of the model over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, and $\varepsilon_1$, $\varepsilon_2$ are sections of $c_1$, $c_2$; `hε₁` states that $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $k$. $D$ is a relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ designation for the model over $A$ (a scheme with a structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a zero section), `hrep` asserts that $D$ represents the subfunctor of rigidified line bundles which are fibrewise algebraically equivalent to zero (the condition `algEquivZeroCut`) with respect to $\varepsilon$, and `hsm`, `hsep` assert that the structure morphism of $D$ is smooth and separated. The hypothesis `hreps` asserts the corresponding representability over $k$ for the base change `D.baseChange k`, and `hPk` that its Poincaré bundle is isomorphic to the base change along $A \to k$ of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep.some` along the first projection of `pullback D.toBase (specMap A k)`. Likewise $D_1$, $D_2$ are relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ designations for $c_1$, $c_2$ over $k$, with representability witnesses `hrep₁`, `hrep₂`.
--
--   **The second component map.** $\nu_2$ is a morphism from the base of `D.baseChange k` to the base of $D_2$ over $\operatorname{Spec} k$, and `hν₂` characterises it: for every $k$-scheme $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and every point $a$ of `(D.baseChange k).toBase` over $t$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep₂.some` along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ is isomorphic to the rigidification (in the sense of `Scheme.Modules.rigidify`, along the section `rigSection c₂ t ε₂` and the projection to $T$) of the pullback along `curveChange i₂.1 i₂.2 t` of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hreps` along $a$.
--
--   **The geometric generic fibre.** Compatible $A$- and $L$-algebra structures on $\overline{\mathbb Q} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` are fixed. $M\eta$ is a curve model over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182), that is, a proper smooth integral curve together with a ring isomorphism of that field with its function field, a bijection between closed points and places, and the compatibilities recorded in `CurveModel`. The morphism $e\eta$ is an isomorphism from $M\eta.C$ to the base change of the two-chart model to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, compatible with the structure morphisms (`heη`). The instance `Mη_chart_nonempty` says the preimage under $e\eta$ followed by the first projection of the image of the finite chart is nonempty, and `hMηpin` says that for every element $a$ of the finite chart algebra [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.chartAlgFin A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L135) the corresponding germ at the generic point, transported by `Mη.ffEquiv.symm`, has Laurent expansion the coefficientwise image under $L \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the expansion of $a$ in $K$. The hypothesis `hgal` is the Galois equivariance of the dictionary: for every $\mathbb Q$-automorphism $g$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ fixing the image of $L$ pointwise and every two $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points $x$, $x'$ of $M\eta$ whose images in the two-chart model satisfy $x' = \operatorname{Spec}(g) \circ x$ (in the composite spelled out there), one has `Mη.pointEquivPlace x' = ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p)) g • Mη.pointEquivPlace x`.
--
--   **Néron special fibre group data.** $G$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9): abelian groups `G.J0s`, `G.JI`, `G.JE`, a subgroup `G.torus` of `G.J0s`, and a surjective homomorphism `G.proj : G.J0s →+ G.JI × G.JE` whose kernel is `G.torus`. The bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` identify `G.J0s`, `G.JI`, `G.JE` with the $k$-points of the bases of `D.baseChange k`, $D_1$, $D_2$ respectively; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` assert that each of these bijections is additive in the sense that the Poincaré bundle pulled back along the point attached to a sum is isomorphic to the tensor product of the two pullbacks; and `hproj` asserts, for each $x$ in `G.J0s`, that `ptsI (G.proj x).1` is `pts x` post-composed with `RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom i₁.1 i₁.2 hε₁ hreps hrep₁.some` and that `ptsE (G.proj x).2` is `pts x` post-composed with $\nu_2$.
--
--   **Igusa models of the components.** $w$ is an [`ModularCurve.IntegralWeightOneForm k M`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L16) (a weight-one form on $\Gamma_1(M)$ with integral $q$-expansion whose reduction constant is nonzero), and $\mathrm{Mdl}_1$, $\mathrm{Mdl}_2$ are curve models over $k$ of the Igusa function field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C k M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35), with isomorphisms $e_1 : \mathrm{Mdl}_1.C \cong C_1$ and $e_2 : \mathrm{Mdl}_2.C \cong C_2$ compatible with the structure morphisms (`he₁`, `he₂`).
--
--   **Generic dictionary and Abel–Jacobi data.** `gpts` is a bijection between [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), the degree-zero divisor class group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182), and the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points of the base of $D$ over `specMap A (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)`, and `hgadd` asserts that it is a homomorphism for the relative group law attached to `hrep.some`. The hypotheses `hDL`, `ajL`, `kL`, `ajbar`, `εbar`, `hPL`, `hajLε`, `hajL`, `hkL₁`, `hkL₂`, `hajbar`, `hajbar_over`, `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj`, `hpts_aj` supply, over $L$ and over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$: representability of `D.baseChange L` with the comparison `hPL` of Poincaré bundles; an Abel–Jacobi morphism `ajL` from the base-changed curve over $L$ to the base of `D.baseChange L` sending the section to the zero section (`hajLε`) and characterised by `hajL`, namely that for every field $K'$, every $t : \operatorname{Spec} K' \to \operatorname{Spec} L$ and every point $x$ of the curve over $t$, the Poincaré bundle pulled back along $x$ followed by `ajL` is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of the section, i.e. to $\mathcal O(x - \varepsilon)$; a comparison morphism `kL` between the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$- and $L$-base changes of the model commuting with both projections (`hkL₁`, `hkL₂`); the composite `ajbar` of $e\eta$, `kL`, `ajL` and the first projection (`hajbar`), lying over the base (`hajbar_over`); a $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-point `εbar` of $M\eta$ lying over $\varepsilon$ (`hεbar`) and killed by `ajbar` (`hεbar_aj`); and `hpts_aj`, which states that for all $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points $x$, $s$ of $M\eta$ with $s$ lying over $\varepsilon$ there is a degree-zero divisor $Dv$ on [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) equal to $[\,$place of $x\,] - [\,$place of $s\,]$ with `gpts (Pic0.mk Dv)` equal to $x$ followed by `ajbar`.
--
--   **Valuation-theoretic data.** $Pl$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $p$ in its set of nonunits (`hPl`, the predicate `LiesOverPrime p`); $\rho : A \to Pl$ is a ring homomorphism inducing the structure map $A \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$ (`hρ`); $O$ is a subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ contained in $Pl$ (`hO`) with a homomorphism $\rho_O : A \to O$ again inducing $A \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$ (`hρO`); and $\pi_k : Pl \to k$ is a surjective ring homomorphism (`hπk`) with $\pi_k \circ \rho$ the structure map $A \to k$ (`hAlgk`).
--
--   **The Weil datum and the integral points.** $m$ is a positive natural number with $p \nmid m$, and $d$ is a Weil datum of order $m$ on $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182): divisors $d.D_1$, $d.D_2$ and nonzero functions $d.f_1$, $d.f_2$ with $\operatorname{ord}_v(d.f_i) = m \cdot d.D_i(v)$ at every place $v$, the supports of $d.D_1$ and $d.D_2$ disjoint, and every place in either support rational. $E_1$, $E_2$ are degree-zero divisors whose underlying divisors are $d.D_1$ and $d.D_2$ (`hE₁`, `hE₂`). The points $z_1$, $z_2$ of the base of $D$ over $\operatorname{Spec}(\rho_O)$ are $O$-integral models of the classes of $E_1$ and $E_2$: `hz₁`, `hz₂` assert that `gpts (Pic0.mk Eᵢ)` equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the inclusion $O \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb Q}$ followed by $z_i$.
--
--   **The reduction hypothesis.** $y_1$ is an element of `G.J0s` whose associated $k$-point, composed with the first projection of `pullback D.toBase (specMap A k)`, equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\pi_k$ composed with the inclusion $O \subseteq Pl$, followed by $z_1$ (`hy₁`); that is, $y_1$ is the reduction of $z_1$. Finally `htor` asserts $G.\mathrm{proj}\,y_1 = 0$, equivalently that $y_1$ lies in `G.torus`.
--
--   **Conclusion.** $d.\mathrm{pairing} = 1$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, that is, $\;\mathrm{evalFun}(d.f_1, d.D_2) / \mathrm{evalFun}(d.f_2, d.D_1) = 1$.
--
--   This is Grothendieck's orthogonality theorem for the $m$-torsion of the Néron special fibre, in the form needed for $J_1(Mp)$ at $p$: a class whose specialisation lies in the toric part (the kernel of the projection to the two Igusa Jacobians) pairs trivially, under the explicit divisorial Weil pairing of a Weil datum of order $m$ prime to $p$, with any class admitting an integral model. It feeds the construction of the free quotient of the special fibre and the specialisation statements for $X_1(Mp)$ used later in the level-lowering argument.
